<!doctype linuxdoc system>

<article>
<title>816os specific information for cc65
<author><url url="https://github.com/kinthelt" name="kinthelt">

<abstract>
An overview of the 816os runtime system as it is implemented for the cc65 C
compiler.
</abstract>

<!-- Table of contents -->
<toc>

<!-- Begin the document -->

<sect>Overview<p>

816os is a preemptive multitasking operating system for a W65C816S
breadboard computer. It runs up to seven programs at once, one in each of the
RAM banks &dollar;01 to &dollar;07, plus a shell in ROM. This file describes
how C programs built with <tt/-t 816os/ run as 816os processes.

Please note that 816os specific functions are just mentioned here, they are
described in the <tt/816os.h/ header file.

<sect>Binary format<p>

The output is a raw memory image, linked to run at &dollar;0000 of whatever
bank the kernel puts it in. Build it with:

<tscreen><verb>
cl65 -t 816os -o hello.bin hello.c
</verb></tscreen>

There are two ways to run it:

<itemize>
<item>Send it to the machine with XMODEM from the shell, for example
      <tt/load 30000/ to load it into bank 3, then start it with <tt/run 3/.
<item>Build it into the ROM, in a program table like the one in 816os's
      <tt>apps/demo_table.s</tt>, and start it with <tt>run &lt;name&gt;</tt>.
</itemize>

<sect>Memory layout<p>

A program owns its whole bank. The kernel starts it at &dollar;0000 in native
mode, with the data bank register set to that bank. cc65 code runs with 8-bit
registers; the startup code switches to them.

<descrip>
  <tag/Program/
  The code, read-only data and initialized data start at &dollar;0000,
  followed by the BSS.

  <tag/C stack/
  The C runtime stack ends at &dollar;DFFF and grows downwards. It is 2 KB
  by default; set <tt/__STACKSIZE__/ to change that.

  <tag/&dollar;E000-&dollar;FFFF/
  Left free, like in the configuration that 816os uses for its own
  programs.

  <tag/Heap/
  The C heap starts at the end of the program and grows towards the C
  stack.

  <tag/Zero page/
  The process's own 256-byte direct page in bank 0. The kernel picks its
  place, and the CPU's direct page register points at it.

  <tag/Hardware stack/
  The process's own 2 KB stack in bank 0.

</descrip><p>

<sect>Platform specific header files<p>

Programs containing 816os specific code may use the <tt/816os.h/ header file.
It declares functions for the kernel services:

<itemize>
<item><tt/os816_putc/, <tt/os816_puts/ and <tt/os816_getc/ for raw console
      I/O.
<item><tt/os816_con_lock/ and <tt/os816_con_unlock/ to keep other processes'
      output out of yours.
<item><tt/os816_yield/, <tt/os816_getpid/ and <tt/os816_ticks/.
<item><tt/os816_load/ and <tt/os816_save/ to transfer memory with XMODEM/CRC.
<item><tt/os816_exec/ to start the program in another bank.
</itemize>

Assembly programs may include <tt/816os.inc/, which has the addresses of the
kernel services. Call them with <tt/JSL/.

<sect>Loadable drivers<p>

There are no loadable drivers for 816os: no graphics, joystick, mouse,
extended memory or RS232 drivers.

<sect>Limitations<p>

<sect1>Console I/O<p>

stdin, stdout and stderr all go to the console:

<itemize>
<item>Output turns '\n' into CR LF. Each <tt/write()/ call holds the console
      lock while it prints, so another process's output can't land in the
      middle of it. A single <tt/printf()/ can make several <tt/write()/
      calls, though. To keep a whole line together, put
      <tt/os816_con_lock()/ and <tt/os816_con_unlock()/ around it.
      These two calls nest, unlike the kernel's lock, so don't call
      <tt/K_CON_LOCK/ directly from a C program.
<item>Input is read a line at a time and echoed. Backspace and Delete erase
      the last character, and Return ends the line. Ctrl-D on an empty line
      is end-of-file. Only the foreground process gets input; a background
      process that reads waits until it is brought to the foreground.
</itemize>

conio is not available.

<sect1>Disk I/O<p>

816os has no file system, so the C file I/O functions such as <tt/fopen/
and <tt/remove/ are not available.

<sect1>Time<p>

<tt/clock()/ counts the kernel's timer ticks, <tt/CLOCKS_PER_SEC/ (200) per
second. The kernel only hands out the low 16 bits of its tick count, so
<tt/clock()/ has to be called at least once every 65536 ticks (about five
and a half minutes) to keep its count right. <tt/sleep()/ gives the CPU to
other processes while it waits. There is no real-time clock, so
<tt/time()/ is not available.

<sect1>Program arguments<p>

816os doesn't pass arguments to programs, so <tt/argc/ is 0 and
<tt/argv[0]/ is a NULL pointer.

<sect1>Exit codes<p>

The kernel has nowhere to put a program's exit code, so the value returned
from <tt/main()/ or passed to <tt/exit()/ is dropped.

<sect1>Running a program again<p>

The kernel doesn't reload a program that is started with
<tt>run &lt;bank&gt;</tt>. If a program that was loaded that way is run a
second time, its initialized variables start with the values they had when
the last run ended. Load it again first. Programs in ROM are copied afresh
each time they are run.

<sect1>Zero page and the direct page<p>

The direct page is in bank 0, but code, data and the C stack are in the
program's own bank. The 65816 adds the direct page register only to
direct page addresses. Absolute addresses use the data bank register. Code
that reaches a zero page variable through an absolute address misses it and
hits the start of the program's bank instead, which is the program's code.
The runtime library avoids this. In your own code, watch for:

<itemize>
<item><tt/lda zp,y/ and the other instructions that have no direct page,Y
      form: the assembler silently makes them absolute,Y. The same applies
      to C code that indexes an array declared in the zero page with
      <tt/#pragma zpsym/.
<item><tt/jmp (zp)/: the 65816 always reads the pointer from bank 0,
      without adding the direct page register.
</itemize>

<sect1>Other<p>

<itemize>
<item>The debugger (<tt/dbg.h/) and dynamically loaded modules (<tt/modload.h/)
      don't work.
<item><tt/_sys()/ loads the status register from its argument, which also
      sets the 65816's register widths. Keep bits 4 and 5 set.
<item>Interrupts belong to the kernel. A program that disables them stops
      every other process.
</itemize>

